    "## Measuring a qubit system  *(Book §7.2, Born Rule, Examples 7.6–7.9)*\n",
    "\n",
    "Amplitudes only become physical through **measurement**. The **Born rule** says $|q\\rangle=x|0\\rangle+y|1\\rangle$ reads $0$ with probability $|x|^2$ and $1$ with probability $|y|^2$, after which the qubit is destroyed — so a state can only be estimated from many reads.\n",
    "\n",
    "`Statevector` measures directly, with **no circuit and no backend**: `probabilities_dict()` gives the exact Born-rule probabilities, `sample_counts(n)` simulates $n$ reads, and `measure()` performs a single read, returning the outcome *and* the collapsed state. `seed()` makes any of it reproducible.\n",
    "\n",
    "Two things the chapter stresses appear below. First, a **global phase is invisible**: $\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}|0\\rangle+\\tfrac12|1\\rangle$ and $\\tfrac{i\\sqrt3}{2}|0\\rangle-\\tfrac12|1\\rangle$ are different vectors with identical measurement statistics. Second, **partial measurement**: `measure(qargs=[k])` reads one qubit and renormalizes the rest. Qiskit is little-endian, so `qargs=[1]` is the **left** (most significant) qubit and `qargs=[0]` the **right** (least significant) — matching the chapter's numbering. We reproduce the four collapsed states $|\\psi_1\\rangle$–$|\\psi_4\\rangle$ of Example 7.9 and check them numerically."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "m5a6b7c8",
   "metadata": {
    "execution": {
