    "## Product and entangled states  *(Book §7.8, Examples 7.17–7.19)*\n",
    "\n",
    "Every tensor product of two qubits is a valid two-qubit system — but the converse fails. A state $|\\psi\\rangle=x_0|00\\rangle+x_1|01\\rangle+x_2|10\\rangle+x_3|11\\rangle$ factors as $|q_1\\rangle\\otimes|q_0\\rangle$ **if and only if** $x_0x_3-x_1x_2=0$; otherwise it is **entangled**. Counting explains why entanglement must exist: a product state is pinned down by $2n$ amplitudes, while a general $n$-qubit state needs $2^n$.\n",
    "\n",
    "We evaluate the criterion on the chapter's product state, its entangled state, and all four Bell states, then confirm the product state really does factor. The last block shows why this matters: in a Bell state, measuring the **left** qubit alone forces the right one — the partial-measurement machinery of §7.2 applied to a state that cannot be separated."
   ]
  },
